{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c769","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0777b-0bfc-71c2-a449-63245f4fe13c"},"sequence":527,"recorded_at":1780531400001049,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56"},"call_id":"call_01a0777c390a7f30908a6e5ed4728716","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/redact.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"strings\"\n5|\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n6|)\n7|\n8|// Redaction markers. The corpus and key-pattern arms share one marker so\n9|// arm order cannot leak which rule fired; the private-key arm names its\n10|// class because a redacted key block must still read as a key block to an\n11|// operator deciding whether to re-enter it.\n12|const (\n13|\tredactedMarker        = \"[redacted]\"\n14|\tredactedPrivateKey    = \"[redacted private key block]\"\n15|\tminSecretRunes        = 8\n16|\tprivateKeyBeginPrefix = \"-----BEGIN \"\n17|\tprivateKeyBeginSuffix = \"PRIVATE KEY-----\"\n18|\tprivateKeyEndPrefix   = \"-----END \"\n19|)\n20|\n21|// sensitiveKeyNames is the reviewed exact-match list for the key=value arm\n22|// below, derived from the Section 16.2 exclusion table (provider\n23|// credentials, SSH/private identity, environment secrets, machine\n24|// authentication). Matching is exact on the lowercased key, never by\n25|// substring: refusing every key containing \"token\" would refuse\n26|// \"token_count\" while passing a secret written under an innocuous name,\n27|// which is pure false-positive surface with no true-positive capability.\n28|//\n29|// Deliberately absent: bare \"key\" (cache keys, sort keys, key=value\n30|// identifiers are ordinary diagnostics), bare \"auth\" (auth status and\n31|// authority digests are not secrets), and \"pwd\"/\"PWD\" (working-directory\n32|// diagnostics are not secrets). Compound keys outside this list (for\n33|// example \"db_password\") are not matched: the corpus arm covers secret\n34|// values the caller knows, and no static list can close the naming\n35|// space. That residue is stated, not hidden.\n36|var sensitiveKeyNames = map[string]bool{\n37|\t\"access_token\": true, \"api_key\": true, \"apikey\": true,\n38|\t\"auth_json\": true, \"authorization\": true, \"auth_token\": true,\n39|\t\"bearer_token\": true, \"client_secret\": true, \"cookie\": true,\n40|\t\"cookies\": true, \"credential\": true, \"credentials\": true,\n41|\t\"dotenv\": true, \"env_secret\": true, \"environment_secret\": true,\n42|\t\"oauth_token\": true, \"passphrase\": true, \"password\": true,\n43|\t\"passwd\": true, \"private_key\": true, \"refresh_token\": true,\n44|\t\"secret\": true, \"secrets\": true, \"session_token\": true,\n45|\t\"ssh_private_key\": true, \"subscription_token\": true, \"token\": true,\n46|}\n47|\n48|// Redact scrubs free text before persistence or display (Section 16.4:\n49|// plugin stderr, provider logs, and doctor output are redacted before\n50|// persistence). It decides exactly three shapes, in this order:\n51|//\n52|//  1. private-key blocks: -----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY----- through the\n53|//     matching -----END ...----- (or to end of input when unterminated,\n54|//     failing closed) become \"[redacted private key block]\";\n55|//  2. caller-known secret values: every secrets entry of at least 8 runes\n56|//     is replaced verbatim with \"[redacted]\". Shorter values are skipped:\n57|//     a two-character secret would redact every occurrence of a common\n58|//     bigram, which is pure false-positive surface. ULID/UUID-shaped\n59|//     non-secrets the caller lists are the caller's error and are\n60|//     redacted all the same;\n61|//  3. sensitive key=value pairs: key=, key:, key =, or \"key\": \" shapes\n62|//     whose key exactly matches sensitiveKeyNames (case-insensitively)\n63|//     have their value replaced with \"[redacted]\", plus URL userinfo\n64|//     (scheme://...@) whose credentials are never displayable.\n65|//\n66|// Section 16.2 is explicit that v0.5.0 does not claim reliable\n67|// content-level secret scrubbing, and this function claims no more: a\n68|// secret under an unlisted key, a bare credential with no key shape, or a\n69|// key block without its header passes through, and only the corpus arm\n70|// can catch those when the caller knows the value. The function is\n71|// idempotent: its markers contain no sensitive key shape, so rescanning\n72|// redacted text changes nothing.\n73|func Redact(line string, secrets []string) string {\n74|\tredacted := redactPrivateKeyBlocks(line)\n75|\tredacted = redactCorpusValues(redacted, secrets)\n76|\tredacted = redactSensitivePairs(redacted)\n77|\tredacted = redactURLUserinfo(redacted)\n78|\treturn redacted\n79|}\n80|\n81|// redactPrivateKeyBlocks replaces PEM private-key blocks. The header must\n82|// contain PRIVATE KEY so certificates and public keys (which are routinely\n83|// logged for pinning) keep passing through.\n84|func redactPrivateKeyBlocks(line string) string {\n85|\tvar rendered strings.Builder\n86|\trendered.Grow(len(line))\n87|\trest := line\n88|\tfor {\n89|\t\tbegin := strings.Index(rest, privateKeyBeginPrefix)\n90|\t\tif begin < 0 {\n91|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(rest)\n92|\t\t\treturn rendered.String()\n93|\t\t}\n94|\t\t// The header close is searched past the opening dashes: the\n95|\t\t// prefix itself starts with dashes, which are not the close.\n96|\t\theaderClose := strings.Index(rest[begin+len(privateKeyBeginPrefix):], \"-----\")\n97|\t\tif headerClose < 0 {\n98|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(rest)\n99|\t\t\treturn rendered.String()\n100|\t\t}\n101|\t\theader := rest[begin : begin+len(privateKeyBeginPrefix)+headerClose+len(\"-----\")]\n102|\t\tif !strings.Contains(header, privateKeyBeginSuffix) {\n103|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(rest[:begin+len(header)])\n104|\t\t\trest = rest[begin+len(header):]\n105|\t\t\tcontinue\n106|\t\t}\n107|\t\tend := strings.Index(rest[begin+len(header):], privateKeyEndPrefix)\n108|\t\tif end < 0 {\n109|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(rest[:begin])\n110|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(redactedPrivateKey)\n111|\t\t\treturn rendered.String()\n112|\t\t}\n113|\t\t// Same dashPrefix care for the END header close.\n114|\t\tafterEnd := rest[begin+len(header)+end+len(privateKeyEndPrefix):]\n115|\t\tcloseDashes := strings.Index(afterEnd, \"-----\")\n116|\t\tif closeDashes < 0 {\n117|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(rest[:begin])\n118|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(redactedPrivateKey)\n119|\t\t\treturn rendered.String()\n120|\t\t}\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c76b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0777b-0bfc-71c2-a449-63245f4fe13c"},"sequence":528,"recorded_at":1780531400001051,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56"},"call_id":"call_01a0777c399671f3877ed329f3f50488","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/render.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"strings\"\n5|)\n6|\n7|// Escape control symbols rendered visibly.\n8|const (\n9|\t// escapeSymbol replaces ESC so ANSI CSI/OSC sequences become inert yet\n10|\t// visible: the byte the terminal would interpret is gone, and the\n11|\t// operator can still see that something was neutralized.\n12|\tescapeSymbol = \"␛\" // U+241B SYMBOL FOR ESCAPE\n13|\t// neutralizedSymbol replaces every other refused control: C0/C1\n14|\t// controls, DEL, bidi overrides, and zero-width format characters.\n15|\tneutralizedSymbol = \"�\" // U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER\n16|)\n17|\n18|// EscapeForTerminal renders untrusted text inert for terminal output\n19|// (Section 16.7: TUI and CLI rendering neutralizes ANSI/OSC sequences,\n20|// control characters, bidi overrides, invalid encodings, and hostile-width\n21|// graphemes before terminal output). The rule per rune:\n22|//\n23|//   - '\\n' and '\\t' pass through: multiline human output needs them, and\n24|//     neither starts an escape sequence or reorders text;\n25|//   - ESC becomes U+241B, so CSI, OSC, and every other ESC-led sequence\n26|//     loses its introducer while staying visible;\n27|//   - every other C0 control, DEL, and every C1 control (including the\n28|//     single-byte CSI U+009B) becomes U+FFFD;\n29|//   - bidi overrides and isolates (U+202A-U+202E, U+2066-U+2069), the\n30|//     explicit directional marks (U+200E, U+200F, U+061C), and the\n31|//     zero-width characters (U+200B-U+200D, U+FEFF) become U+FFFD: they\n32|//     reorder or hide text without showing anything;\n33|//   - invalid UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD per run, so the result is always valid\n34|//     UTF-8 and no raw byte reaches the terminal;\n35|//   - every other rune passes through unchanged.\n36|//\n37|// The function is idempotent: every symbol it emits is a fixed point, so\n38|// escaping already-escaped output changes nothing and double-wiring a\n39|// render path cannot corrupt text.\n40|//\n41|// Stated bounds. Soft hyphen (U+00AD), combining-mark floods, emoji\n42|// presentation sequences, and other grapheme-width anomalies are not\n43|// measured: neutralizing them needs terminal width tables this package\n44|// does not carry, and stripping combining marks would mangle legitimate\n45|// non-Latin text. Full-width spoofing beyond the enumerated invisible\n46|// controls is therefore unproven and named here rather than claimed.\n47|func EscapeForTerminal(text string) string {\n48|\ttext = strings.ToValidUTF8(text, neutralizedSymbol)\n49|\tvar rendered strings.Builder\n50|\trendered.Grow(len(text))\n51|\tfor _, character := range text {\n52|\t\tswitch {\n53|\t\tcase character == '\\n' || character == '\\t':\n54|\t\t\trendered.WriteRune(character)\n55|\t\tcase character == 0x1b:\n56|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(escapeSymbol)\n57|\t\tcase character < 0x20 || character == 0x7f || character >= 0x80 && character <= 0x9f:\n58|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(neutralizedSymbol)\n59|\t\tcase character >= 0x202a && character <= 0x202e,\n60|\t\t\tcharacter >= 0x2066 && character <= 0x2069,\n61|\t\t\tcharacter == 0x200e || character == 0x200f || character == 0x061c,\n62|\t\t\tcharacter >= 0x200b && character <= 0x200d,\n63|\t\t\tcharacter == 0xfeff:\n64|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(neutralizedSymbol)\n65|\t\tdefault:\n66|\t\t\trendered.WriteRune(character)\n67|\t\t}\n68|\t}\n69|\treturn rendered.String()\n70|}\n71|\n72|// RenderForTerminal prepares one untrusted line for terminal display:\n73|// secret scrubbing first (so a secret containing an escape sequence is\n74|// removed as a secret, not merely neutralized), then control escaping.\n75|// secrets carries the exact secret values the caller knows; nil admits no\n76|// corpus redaction while key-pattern and private-key scrubbing still\n77|// apply. The composition is idempotent because both stages are.\n78|func RenderForTerminal(line string, secrets []string) string {\n79|\treturn EscapeForTerminal(Redact(line, secrets))\n80|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c76d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0777b-0bfc-71c2-a449-63245f4fe13c"},"sequence":529,"recorded_at":1780531400001053,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56"},"call_id":"call_01a0777c3a4c7ce1ba5395f8ad9736e6","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/nofollow.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"os\"\n6|\t\"strings\"\n7|)\n8|\n9|// errIsSymlink reports the Windows Lstat gate refusal. It stays internal:\n10|// callers see the failPath refusal, and errors.Is against ErrUnsafePath\n11|// is the supported match.\n12|var errIsSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink refused before open\")\n13|\n14|// errCommitSymlink is the internal signal from the platform commit walk\n15|// (openCommitRelative) that a symlink or reparse point stands on the\n16|// member path at commit time. It stays internal: Open maps it to the\n17|// failPath \"member symlink escape\" refusal, and errors.Is against\n18|// ErrUnsafePath is the supported match. The walk returns no failPath\n19|// sites of its own so the refusal inventory stays in shared code,\n20|// which the suite executes on every platform it runs on.\n21|var errCommitSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink on the commit path\")\n22|\n23|// guardRootMatches reports whether the open directory handle refers to\n24|// the same directory as rootPath: the handle's fstat identity against a\n25|// stat of the path (device and inode on unix via os.SameFile, volume\n26|// and file index on Windows). A stat failure on either side is not a\n27|// match: an unreadable root fails closed into the mismatch refusal\n28|// rather than admitting an unverifiable handle.\n29|func guardRootMatches(root *os.File, rootPath string) bool {\n30|\thandleInfo, err := root.Stat()\n31|\tif err != nil || handleInfo == nil {\n32|\t\treturn false\n33|\t}\n34|\tpathInfo, err := os.Stat(rootPath)\n35|\tif err != nil || pathInfo == nil {\n36|\t\treturn false\n37|\t}\n38|\treturn os.SameFile(handleInfo, pathInfo)\n39|}\n40|\n41|// OpenNoFollowFile opens an existing regular file read-only, refusing a\n42|// trailing symlink instead of traversing it (Section 16.3\n43|// \"symlink/reparse escape during both validation and commit\"). The\n44|// descriptor refers to the opened inode, and the post-open Fstat below\n45|// reads that descriptor rather than the path, so a swap between open and\n46|// stat cannot retarget the check: only the final-component open itself\n47|// races, and on unix O_NOFOLLOW closes even that.\n48|//\n49|// The file must be regular: directories, devices, pipes, and sockets are\n50|// refused after opening (and the descriptor closed) rather than handed\n51|// back. Parent directories are the caller's responsibility: open the\n52|// staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and commit members through\n53|// Guard.Open, which binds the walk to the root handle. Composing\n54|// Guard.Resolve with this function instead is not contained: Resolve\n55|// answers the lexical question only, and a symlinked intermediate\n56|// directory redirects a path-string open outside the root.\n57|func OpenNoFollowFile(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n58|\tfile, err := openNoFollow(path)\n59|\tif err != nil {\n60|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n61|\t}\n62|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n63|\tif err != nil {\n64|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n65|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n66|\t}\n67|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n68|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n69|\t\treturn nil, err\n70|\t}\n71|\t// The unix open sets O_NONBLOCK so a FIFO can never stall the opener\n72|\t// (its refusal above runs only after the open returns). A regular\n73|\t// file ignores O_NONBLOCK on reads, and clearing it here keeps a\n74|\t// borrowed descriptor from leaking the flag to its reader. fcntl on\n75|\t// a just-opened valid descriptor cannot fail; the best-effort clear\n76|\t// carries no refusal site for that reason.\n77|\t_ = clearNonblock(file)\n78|\treturn file, nil\n79|}\n80|\n81|// Open commits one guard member to an open regular file through the\n82|// staging-root directory handle, refusing symlink and reparse escape at\n83|// commit time (Section 16.3 \"symlink/reparse escape during both\n84|// validation and commit\").\n85|//\n86|// Validation and commit are two halves of one entry point, not two calls\n87|// the caller composes: the member grammar and the managed set refuse\n88|// lexically first, then the platform walk (openCommitRelative) opens\n89|// every path component relative to a directory descriptor — never by\n90|// re-resolving a path string — so a symlinked intermediate directory\n91|// cannot redirect the commit outside the root the way composing\n92|// Guard.Resolve with OpenNoFollowFile does (O_NOFOLLOW constrains only\n93|// the final component; every component before it is still followed by\n94|// the kernel when a path string is opened).\n95|//\n96|// The caller opens the staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and passes the\n97|// handle: on unix the walk never leaves that handle (openat from the\n98|// root descriptor down, O_NOFOLLOW per component, TOCTOU-closed); on\n99|// Windows the walk is a best-effort Lstat component check with the\n100|// stated check-then-open bound. The returned file is cleared of\n101|// O_NONBLOCK like OpenNoFollowFile. The caller owns closing both the\n102|// root handle and the returned file.\n103|//\n104|// The handle is bound to the guard root before descending:\n105|// guardRootMatches fstats the handle and compares device and inode\n106|// against a stat of guard.root, refusing a foreign directory handle\n107|// with \"guard root mismatch\". The check lives here in shared code so\n108|// both platforms answer the mismatch identically; past it, the unix\n109|// walk descends from the verified handle and the Windows walk from the\n110|// verified root path, which name the same directory.\n111|func (guard Guard) Open(root *os.File, member string) (*os.File, error) {\n112|\tif err := CheckMemberPath(guard.platform, member); err != nil {\n113|\t\treturn nil, err\n114|\t}\n115|\tif guard.checkManaged && !guard.managed[member] {\n116|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"member unmanaged\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n117|\t}\n118|\tif root == nil {\n119|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"guard root handle\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n120|\t}\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
