{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000caeb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0777b-0bfc-71c2-a449-63245f4fe13c"},"sequence":976,"recorded_at":1780531400001947,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"32f7efe8-067d-4d98-a2af-fff5c474ce56"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"28ff8ce8-1466-42c8-83a8-471d5b18e1a7"},"task_id":"28ff8ce8-1466-42c8-83a8-471d5b18e1a7","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"28ff8ce8-1466-42c8-83a8-471d5b18e1a7","chunk":"Read text file `internal/secprim/errors.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"fmt\"\n6|)\n7|\n8|// Refusal sentinels. Every refusal this package produces wraps exactly one\n9|// of these, so callers match on the kind without parsing human text.\n10|var (\n11|\tErrUnsafePath = errors.New(\"secprim unsafe path\")\n12|\tErrUnsafeArgv = errors.New(\"secprim unsafe argv\")\n13|\tErrUnsafeEnv  = errors.New(\"secprim unsafe environment\")\n14|)\n15|\n16|// Error is the only refusal this package produces. Kind is one of the\n17|// sentinels above; Rule names the specification rule that fired\n18|// (for example \"member traversal\" or \"argv NUL\"); Detail names the\n19|// offending member, argument position, or variable name without echoing\n20|// foreign bytes beyond what names the site.\n21|type Error struct {\n22|\tKind   error\n23|\tRule   string\n24|\tDetail string\n25|\tcause  error\n26|}\n27|\n28|func (err *Error) Error() string {\n29|\tif err.cause == nil {\n30|\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%v: %s: %s\", err.Kind, err.Rule, err.Detail)\n31|\t}\n32|\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%v: %s: %s: %v\", err.Kind, err.Rule, err.Detail, err.cause)\n33|}\n34|\n35|// WithCause records the underlying filesystem failure for errors.Is and\n36|// errors.As without changing the refusal rendering the witnesses pin:\n37|// the cause appends after the pinned prefix.\n38|func (err *Error) WithCause(cause error) *Error {\n39|\terr.cause = cause\n40|\treturn err\n41|}\n42|\n43|func (err *Error) Unwrap() []error {\n44|\tif err.cause == nil {\n45|\t\treturn []error{err.Kind}\n46|\t}\n47|\treturn []error{err.Kind, err.cause}\n48|}\n49|\n50|// The three refusal constructors below are declared as variables so the\n51|// refusal-inventory gate can observe every exercised refusal site. Each is\n52|// the single construction site for its failure class; production code must\n53|// not build Error values any other way: no aliasing a constructor into a\n54|// second name, no &Error{} or Error{} literal, no new(Error), and no\n55|// wrapper function or method that manufactures an *Error. The inventory\n56|// test enforces every member of that shape space, not just the bare\n57|// constructor spelling.\n58|//\n59|// Stated bound. The gate matches the Error identifier spelling: a\n60|// refusal type spelled through a type alias (type X = Error), a\n61|// defined type (type X Error, even when converted back to *Error), or\n62|// a factory whose result spells the type through such an alias is\n63|// outside the witness. The derivation is syntactic on purpose — it\n64|// runs on parsed source with no type information — so it closes the\n65|// enumerated spellings, not the class. See\n66|// TestRefusalInventorySpellingBoundIsStated, which pins this bound by\n67|// feeding the alias spellings through the derivation and requiring\n68|// the gate to stay silent about them.\n69|//\n70|// failPath reports a Section 16.3 path or filesystem refusal: a member\n71|// outside the contained grammar, a symlink or special file where a\n72|// regular file is required, a case-fold collision, or an unmanaged\n73|// replacement.\n74|//\n75|// failArgv reports a Section 16.4 argv refusal: an empty vector, an empty\n76|// executable or argument element, a NUL byte, or invalid UTF-8.\n77|//\n78|// failEnv reports a Section 16.7 environment refusal: a name outside the\n79|// environment-name grammar, a literal colliding with an inherited name, or\n80|// a NUL byte or invalid UTF-8 in a value.\n81|var failPath = func(rule, detail string) *Error {\n82|\treturn &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafePath, Rule: rule, Detail: detail}\n83|}\n84|\n85|var failArgv = func(rule, detail string) *Error {\n86|\treturn &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafeArgv, Rule: rule, Detail: detail}\n87|}\n88|\n89|var failEnv = func(rule, detail string) *Error {\n90|\treturn &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafeEnv, Rule: rule, Detail: detail}\n91|}"}}}
